  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 327 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern remains largely unchanged this morning, with \nthe mid-level ridge remaining centered over New Mexico. Water vapor \nimagery shows a shortwave over southwestern KS which is generating \ncloud cover and light rain. There is also a band of showers and \nstorms in south-central KS and north-central OK that has developed \njust ahead of a surface low in and area of isentropic ascent. Most \nCAMs show that band of precipitation expanding northward into the \narea toward sunrise as isentropic ascent increases northeast. There \nwill be around 1000 J/kg of MUCAPE, but fairly weak shear as the LLJ \nlooks to stay south of the area. So while thunderstorms are \npossible, severe weather is not expected this morning. Rain and \nstorms should quickly dissipate as the ascent decreases mid-morning. \nTemperatures then warm well into the 80s for much of the area this \nafternoon with heat index values a few degrees higher.\n\nThe next chance for thunderstorms will be Tuesday afternoon and \nevening with models showing another shortwave moving over the area \nwithin the northwest flow aloft. The environment should be more \nfavorable for a few severe storms with around 3000 J/kg of MLCAPE \nand limited CIN. Shear again isn't overly impressive so damaging \nwind gusts appear to be the main concern, especially with DCAPE \naround 1000 J/kg.\n\nA weak surface boundary then moves through northeast KS on Wednesday\nand high pressure expands southward behind it. The mid-level ridge \nlooks to move slightly westward, keeping the track of shortwaves west\nof the area. Thus, dry weather is expected through the end of the \nweek. Temperatures remain fairly consistent each day with lows in the\n60s and highs in the mid to upper 80s through the workweek before \ntemperatures warm further this weekend.",
